/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 2.1 Clause Taxonomy and Four Sentence Architectural Types) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Sentence structure dictates the clarity and logical progression of technical arguments.) Tj
T*
(* Clauses:) Tj
T*
(- Independent \(Main\) Clause: Contains a subject and predicate, expressing a) Tj
T*
(complete thought that can stand alone as a grammatical sentence.) Tj
T*
(- Dependent \(Subordinate\) Clause: Contains a subject and predicate but begins with) Tj
T*
(a subordinating conjunction \(because, although, if, since, when\) or relative) Tj
T*
(pronoun, incapable of standing alone.) Tj
T*
(* The Four Sentence Types:) Tj
T*
(1. Simple Sentence: Exactly one independent clause: "The enzyme catalyzed the substrate) Tj
T*
(at 37 deg C.") Tj
T*
(2. Compound Sentence: Two or more independent clauses joined by a coordinating) Tj
T*
(conjunction \(FANBOYS\) preceded by a comma, or linked by a semicolon: "The reaction) Tj
T*
(reached equilibrium, but the yield remained suboptimal.") Tj
T*
(3. Complex Sentence: One independent clause combined with at least one dependent clause:) Tj
T*
("Although the temperature increased, the reaction rate plateaued because the enzyme) Tj
T*
(denatured.") Tj
T*
(4. Compound-Complex Sentence: At least two independent clauses and one or more dependent) Tj
T*
(clauses: "When the sterile medium was inoculated, cell growth accelerated, and oxygen) Tj
T*
(consumption doubled.") Tj
T*
(* Common Syntactic Errors:) Tj
T*
(- Sentence Fragment: Incomplete thought lacking an independent clause.) Tj
T*
(- Run-On \(Fused\) Sentence: Two independent clauses joined without punctuation or) Tj
T*
(coordinating conjunctions.) Tj
T*
(- Comma Splice: Two independent clauses incorrectly joined by only a comma without) Tj
T*
(a conjunction. Fix: replace comma with a semicolon or add coordinating) Tj
T*
(conjunction.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 2.2 Rules of Subject-Verb Agreement \(Concord\)) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(A finite verb must agree with its grammatical subject in person and number.) Tj
T*
(* Rule 1 \(Intervening Prepositional Phrases\): The verb agrees strictly with the head) Tj
T*
(noun, NOT with intervening nouns inside prepositional phrases:) Tj
T*
("The efficacy of the newly developed synthetic antimicrobial peptides IS remarkable") Tj
T*
(\(Efficacy is singular\).) Tj
T*
(* Rule 2 \(Compound Subjects\): Two singular subjects joined by 'and' take a plural) Tj
T*
(verb: "DNA and RNA are nucleic acids." Exception: When two singular nouns represent a) Tj
T*
(single combined entity: "Bread and butter is his breakfast.") Tj
T*
(* Rule 3 \(Correlative Proximity Rule\): When subjects are connected by 'either...or') Tj
T*
(or 'neither...nor', the verb agrees with the CLOSEST subject:) Tj
T*
ET

